  <p class="iop-essay-label">
    WEEK 07 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>
    4 + 8
  </h3>

  <p>
    I am 35 years old and sitting in a classroom. Spiritual Steve has just handed out a piece of paper as he tells a story of teaching teenagers in a jail setting. He wanted to teach them the book <em>The Four Agreements</em>, but teenage boys don’t read. So he summed it up and put it on one sheet of paper—the piece of paper he just handed us. “Put this on your fridge.” I read it, loved it, and slipped it in my binder. When I got home, I put it on my fridge.
  </p>

  <p>
    I am 34 years old, sitting in a dorm in jail on my bunk. I’m reading <em>The Heart of the Buddha’s Teaching</em> by Thích Nhất Hạnh. Rereading the part about the Eightfold Path again, I’m taking notes on things I missed on the first read-through. I had already gotten through the Four Noble Truths. I was so grateful that the chaplain had given me this book—it was by far my favorite I’d read in jail.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before these times, I was in the dark about how to live. I just floated along, drifting through hallways and existing in rooms with no sense of how to deal with difficult people, moments, and emotions properly. I had no idea what mindfulness or equanimity were. I didn’t know about how attachment leads to craving and craving leads to suffering. I didn’t know about domestication in the sense that it’s the process through which society teaches us what to believe, how to behave, and who we’re supposed to be—often before we’re old enough to question any of it. We inherit beliefs, we’re rewarded and punished, we internalize those rules, and we become our own judges.
  </p>

  <p>
    These concepts are introductory ideas in <em>The Four Agreements</em> by Don Miguel Ruiz and Buddhism. Both come from different philosophical traditions, but they emphasize the importance of awareness, intentional behavior, and changing one’s relationship with suffering. The Four Agreements focus on challenging internalized beliefs and improving how people communicate and interact, while Buddhism explores craving, attachment, and the path toward freedom from suffering. Together, they offer complementary approaches to living more consciously without demanding perfection.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the paper with the summary from <em>The Four Agreements</em>, it said:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Be Impeccable With Your Word</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Speak with integrity.</li>
        <li>Say only what you mean.</li>
        <li>Avoid using the word to speak against yourself or to gossip about others.</li>
        <li>Use the power of your word in the direction of truth and love.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Never Make Assumptions</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Find courage to ask questions and to express what you really want.</li>
        <li>Communicate with others as clearly as you can to avoid misunderstandings, sadness, and drama.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Never Take Anything Personal</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Nothing others do is because of you.</li>
        <li>What others say and do is a projection of their own reality, their own dream.</li>
        <li>When you are immune to the opinions and actions of others, you won’t be the victim of needless suffering.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Always Do Your Best</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Under any circumstance, simply do your best.</li>
        <li>Your best is going to change from moment to moment.</li>
        <li>When you do your best, you avoid self-judgment, self-abuse, and regret.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Sounds easy enough. Putting this into practice is hard. It’s like Buddhism—sounds easy, but it’s hard to live in practice. The simplicity is very deceptive. Each agreement challenges a behavior that has become automatic through years of conditioning. Gossip is a reflex, words hurt, assumptions are facts, and other people’s opinions can become personal judgments. Meanwhile, the expectation of perfection can turn even our best efforts into perceived failures. On paper, these agreements seem ridiculously simple. Unfortunately, humans have a remarkable talent for making simple things unnecessarily complicated.
  </p>

  <p>
    While Don Miguel Ruiz offers four agreements for changing beliefs and behaviors that shape our lives, Buddhism offers four truths about the nature of suffering, known as the Four Noble Truths. They are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>There is suffering.</li>
    <li>There is a cause of suffering, including craving.</li>
    <li>There is an end to suffering.</li>
    <li>There is a path leading to the end of suffering.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    First, we know that suffering exists and is an unavoidable part of the human experience. With the Second Noble Truth, it’s understood that attachment leads to craving, and craving leads to suffering. For example, attachment to a pleasurable feeling—such as drinking—leads to craving, like wanting to drink more. This leads to suffering. Or simply being attached to a happy or pleasurable feeling or thing. Not wanting it to end, the attachment leads to craving it more. This craving leads to suffering. After we can accept suffering, we can have hope that there is an end to suffering. The path to the end of suffering is the Noble Eightfold Path.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Noble Eightfold Path is a practical guide or set of instructions for cultivating wisdom, ethical conduct, and mindfulness:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wisdom:</strong> Understanding and Intention.</li>
    <li><strong>Ethical conduct:</strong> Speech, Action, and Livelihood.</li>
    <li><strong>Mental cultivation:</strong> Effort, Mindfulness, and Concentration.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Knowing why suffering happens isn’t enough; the Eightfold Path provides ways of living that can reduce it. The Path includes practices that instruct changes to ordinary daily life that can make a difference in how someone can reduce their suffering and help others.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is overlap between the Four Agreements and the Eightfold Path. They have different roots, but they overlap in communication, perception, and intentional behavior. The first agreement, <em>Be Impeccable With Your Word</em>, resembles the principle of Wise Speech. Both emphasize the importance of truthfulness, avoiding harmful language, not gossiping, not exaggerating, and considering how words affect ourselves and others. The first agreement encourages people to use their words with integrity rather than as weapons, while Wise Speech emphasizes avoiding lies, divisive speech, and harsh language. In both approaches, communication is more than simply exchanging information—it’s an opportunity to practice awareness, compassion, and personal responsibility.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second agreement is <em>Don’t Take Anything Personally</em>, and it connects with Wise Understanding. The agreement states that other people's words and actions reflect their own beliefs, experiences, and perceptions rather than defining someone else's worth. Buddhist Wise Understanding encourages recognizing that human behavior arises from numerous causes and conditions. Thích Nhất Hạnh's teaching of interbeing expands on this idea by emphasizing that people are interconnected and influenced by their circumstances. Understanding these influences can make it easier to respond to criticism or difficult behavior with perspective rather than immediately taking offense. However, this doesn't mean excusing harmful behavior or abandoning personal boundaries. It means recognizing that another person's actions aren't necessarily a reflection of one's own identity.
  </p>

  <p>
    The third agreement, <em>Don’t Make Assumptions</em>, is similar to Wise Mindfulness. It encourages asking questions and communicating clearly instead of inventing explanations for things we don't understand. Wise Mindfulness involves observing thoughts, emotions, and perceptions without automatically accepting them as accurate representations of reality. The agreement encourages direct communication rather than making assumptions, while mindfulness encourages noticing the assumption before reacting to it. Both approaches create space between an initial thought and the behavior that follows.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, <em>Always Do Your Best</em> parallels Wise Effort. The expectation isn't perfection, but genuine effort without unnecessary self-judgment. Similarly, Wise Effort involves cultivating beneficial mental states, reducing harmful patterns, and continuing to practice even when progress is difficult. Neither philosophy suggests that people must perform flawlessly. Instead, both emphasize consistency, awareness, and the willingness to keep improving.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Four Agreements and the Eightfold Path are not identical teachings, but they complement one another. The agreements focus on replacing inherited beliefs and habitual reactions with consciously chosen agreements, while Buddhism offers a broader approach to understanding suffering through wisdom, ethical conduct, and mental cultivation. Both recognize that thoughts, words, and actions influence how people experience the world. Ultimately, they suggest that living consciously isn't about controlling every circumstance or becoming a perfect human being. It's about recognizing our patterns, questioning our perceptions, and making deliberate choices about how we respond to life.
  </p>

  <p>
    Thích Nhất Hạnh takes these concepts beyond words on paper in a book and puts them into practice through mindfulness and compassion. His teachings on interbeing explain that nothing exists entirely independently: our thoughts, actions, relationships, and experiences are influenced by countless interconnected conditions. This complements the second agreement, <em>Don't Take Anything Personally</em>, by encouraging us to recognize that other people's behavior is shaped by circumstances beyond ourselves. His emphasis on loving speech and deep listening also expands upon Wise Speech and <em>Be Impeccable with Your Word</em>, reminding us that communication involves not only what we say but how we listen. In <em>No Mud, No Lotus</em>, Thích Nhất Hạnh explores how suffering can be understood and transformed rather than avoided or suppressed. Just as a lotus grows from mud, compassion and understanding can emerge from difficult experiences. Mindfulness allows us to recognize our thoughts and emotions without immediately reacting to them, creating an opportunity to respond with greater intention. These teachings demonstrate that philosophical principles aren't simply ideas to memorize; they're practices that influence how we interact with ourselves and others.
  </p>

  <p>
    Just as Thích Nhất Hạnh puts the concepts into practice and provides advice on how to do so in his literature, there is a difference between reading the book and doing the action. You can make flashcards of the vocabulary words for Spanish class, but can you practice enough to ace the test? Neither the Eightfold Path nor the Four Agreements is a checklist. They are handbooks on how to live life that are supposed to influence actions on a daily basis. Batteries not included—they take work.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm currently waiting to have back surgery and am having trouble walking around, so I use a cane. That's what the Four Agreements and the Noble Eightfold Path are—a cane. They help you navigate life and get around. You can get around without one, but it's harder. You might be accustomed to walking on your own, but when you're having trouble, there's nothing wrong with accepting a little assistance. The cane isn't going to walk for you, just as the Four Agreements and Eightfold Path aren't going to live your life for you. You still have to take the steps. So use the damn cane and accept the help.
  </p>

</article>
